[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: Despite attempting to implement comprehensive Pretense state machine, fake synthesis, unit tests, parity verification, and stateful fuzzing for DagEngineLite, multiple contract discrepancies remain in benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py. In TestDagEngineLiteImplReal (lines 835-863, test_real_node_not_found_errors and test_real_validation_and_cycle_errors), testing exception contracts (NodeNotFoundError and CycleError mandated by low/dag_engine_lite.pyi) by wrapping self.sm.dispatch_transition with self.assertRaises fails because dispatch_transition returns a Raised outcome rather than propagating the exception; real SUT exception assertions must be performed directly on SUT instances or by checking the Raised outcome. In DagEngineLiteStateMachine.run (lines 595-656), ctx.update is a no-op that fails to update self.state.node_states from DAGRunReport outcomes, causing subsequent WaveScheduler.get_state postconditions to fail with stale READY states against SUCCEEDED. Additionally, WaveScheduler transitions mark_failed, mark_running, mark_succeeded, and get_state (lines 465-532, 562-580) omit antecedent guards in ctx.requires to verify that target nodes exist in self.state.nodes, permitting invalid dispatch on empty graphs with synthetic identifiers.
[CHANGE] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: Fix exception assertions to test SUT directly, update state node_states upon runner execution, and add antecedent guards for WaveScheduler node existence
[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: While the author attempted to fix exception assertions to test SUT directly, update state node_states upon runner execution, and add antecedent guards for WaveScheduler node existence, a contract discrepancy remains in benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py in DagEngineLiteStateMachine.add_edge (lines 191-203). The transition's update handler imposes an unmandated state demotion by setting self.state.node_states[downstream_id] to NodeState.PENDING when an edge is added to an already-initialized node. In low/dag_engine_lite.pyi and low/dag_engine_lite_impl.pyi, DAG.add_edge only establishes dependency edges and does not mutate scheduler node states or define any backward transition from NodeState.READY to NodeState.PENDING. Consequently, subsequent postcondition checks in WaveScheduler.get_state (lines 573-578) fail because the test model expects NodeState.PENDING instead of the valid NodeState.READY lifecycle state maintained by the scheduler. The author must remove this uncontracted node_states demotion from add_edge's update logic.
[CHANGE] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: Remove uncontracted node_states demotion from add_edge update handler to preserve valid scheduler lifecycle states
[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: While the author implemented concrete DAG, WaveScheduler, and DAGRunner with topological sort, cycle detection, critical path calculation, and retry execution, WaveScheduler._sync_states (lines 337-353) deviates from the dynamic state synchronization contract in low/dag_engine_lite_impl.pyi and the NodeState lifecycle in low/dag_engine_lite.pyi. When a registered node already present in internal scheduler state as NodeState.READY has directed edges added via add_edge, _sync_states only initializes nodes absent from internal state (if nid not in self._states) and fails to update unexecuted nodes to NodeState.PENDING when unsatisfied upstream dependencies exist. This causes read queries to produce contradictory node states depending on whether scheduler methods are invoked before or after add_edge. Unexecuted nodes in the scheduler must dynamically synchronize to NodeState.PENDING whenever unsatisfied upstream dependencies are present in the DAG.
[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: While the author attempted to remove uncontracted node_states demotion from add_edge update handler to preserve valid scheduler lifecycle states, DagEngineLiteStateMachine.add_edge (lines 191-203) fails to synchronize the model's node_states with the NodeState lifecycle mandated by low/dag_engine_lite.pyi. When add_edge adds an upstream dependency to a node that has not executed, the downstream node possesses unsatisfied dependencies and must transition to NodeState.PENDING until those dependencies succeed. Because add_edge's update handler leaves the downstream node in NodeState.READY, subsequent WaveScheduler.get_state postconditions (lines 573-578) fail against the mandated NodeState.PENDING contract. The transition update handler must update node_states for the downstream node to NodeState.PENDING whenever its upstream dependencies are not in NodeState.SUCCEEDED.
[CHANGE] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: Synchronize model node_states on add_edge by transitioning downstream node to PENDING when upstream dependencies are not SUCCEEDED
[CHANGE] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: Dynamically synchronize unexecuted nodes in WaveScheduler._sync_states to NodeState.PENDING when unsatisfied upstream dependencies exist
[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: While the author synchronized model node_states on add_edge by transitioning downstream node to PENDING when upstream dependencies are not SUCCEEDED, a lifecycle state discrepancy remains in benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py in DagEngineLiteStateMachine.remove_node (lines 228-245). When remove_node removes an upstream node, any dependent node whose upstream dependencies become empty or fully satisfied is promoted to a root or unblocked node, which conforms to NodeState.READY under low/dag_engine_lite.pyi. However, remove_node's update handler leaves the downstream node's state in self.state.node_states as NodeState.PENDING. As a result, subsequent postcondition assertions in WaveScheduler.get_state (lines 573-578) fail because the state model expects NodeState.PENDING instead of the valid NodeState.READY state maintained by the scheduler. The author must update remove_node to promote dependent nodes in self.state.node_states to NodeState.READY when all their remaining upstream dependencies are satisfied.
[CHANGE] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: Promote dependent nodes to READY in remove_node update handler when all remaining upstream dependencies are satisfied
[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: While the author promoted dependent nodes to READY in remove_node update handler when all remaining upstream dependencies are satisfied, a lifecycle state discrepancy remains in benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py in DagEngineLiteStateMachine.add_node (lines 147-159). The transition's update handler unconditionally overwrites self.state.node_states[node_id] to NodeState.PENDING or NodeState.READY whenever add_node is invoked. When add_node is called with an identifier for an existing node that has already transitioned to a terminal state (such as NodeState.CANCELLED under FailurePolicy.FAIL_FAST, or NodeState.SUCCEEDED/FAILED mandated by low/dag_engine_lite.pyi), overwriting node_states erroneously resets the scheduler model state, because add_node does not purge or reset terminal scheduler lifecycle tracking. Consequently, subsequent WaveScheduler.get_state postconditions (lines 576-581) fail when asserting NodeState.PENDING against the terminal state preserved by the scheduler. The update handler in add_node must only initialize node_states for a node identifier if node_id is not already present in self.state.node_states.
[CHANGE] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: Only initialize node_states in add_node if node_id is not already present to preserve existing lifecycle states
[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: While the author attempted to only initialize node_states in add_node if node_id is not already present to preserve existing lifecycle states, a contract discrepancy remains in benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py in DagEngineLiteStateMachine.add_node (lines 147-160). When an existing node that is in an unexecuted lifecycle state (NodeState.READY or NodeState.PENDING under low/dag_engine_lite.pyi) is re-registered via add_node with new unsatisfied dependencies, guarding with if node_id not in self.state.node_states completely skips state synchronization, leaving a node with unsatisfied dependencies stuck in NodeState.READY. Under low/dag_engine_lite.pyi, an unexecuted node with unsatisfied dependencies must be in NodeState.PENDING, causing subsequent WaveScheduler.get_state postconditions (lines 576-581) to fail. The update handler in add_node must synchronize non-terminal node_states so that any unexecuted node with unsatisfied dependencies transitions to NodeState.PENDING while preserving immutable terminal states (SUCCEEDED, FAILED, CANCELLED, SKIPPED).
[CHANGE] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: Synchronize non-terminal node_states in add_node update handler to transition unexecuted nodes with unsatisfied dependencies to PENDING while preserving terminal states
[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: While the author attempted to synchronize non-terminal node_states in add_node update handler to transition unexecuted nodes with unsatisfied dependencies to PENDING while preserving terminal states, a lifecycle state discrepancy remains in benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py in DagEngineLiteStateMachine.add_node (lines 155-160). When add_node is invoked for an existing node that is currently in NodeState.RUNNING, checking whether the node is not in terminal_states improperly includes NodeState.RUNNING as an unexecuted state, resetting the actively executing node back to NodeState.PENDING. Under low/dag_engine_lite.pyi, NodeState.RUNNING represents an active in-flight execution state that cannot be demoted back to NodeState.PENDING upon re-registration, which causes subsequent WaveScheduler.get_state postconditions (lines 577-582) to fail. The update handler in add_node must restrict dependency synchronization exclusively to unexecuted states (NodeState.READY, NodeState.PENDING, or uninitialized) and preserve NodeState.RUNNING along with terminal states.
[CHANGE] benchmark_3/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: Restrict add_node dependency synchronization exclusively to unexecuted states (READY, PENDING, or uninitialized) and preserve RUNNING along with terminal states
